r Tiles.\n\nSimilarly, **Middle Tiles**? Needs 4,5,6 \u2014 I have W6, but not enough.\n\nWhat about **Pure Straight**? Needs 123, 456, 789 chows in same suit.\n\nI have W678 \u2014 close to 789, but missing 7 and 9? W678 is 6,7,8 \u2014 not 789.\n\nAnd I have W9s \u2014 could go for W789 chow? But I don\u2019t have W7 or W8 free \u2014 they\u2019re used in W678.\n\nUnless I break it \u2014 but too late.\n\nAlternatively, consider **All Pungs** \u2014 I have one pung (T999), two chows \u2014 not favorable.\n\nBut note: I have **three chows and one pung**, plus singletons.\n\nPossibility: **All Chows** (2 points) if I turn T999 into something? No, it's fixed.\n\nWait \u2014 **T999** is a pung \u2014 so I cannot have All Chows.\n\nSo **not All Chows**.\n\nCould I go for **Half Flush**?\n\nHalf Flush: only one suit of numbers + honors.\n\nCurrently, I have **Bamboo**, **Characters**, and **Dots (B2)** \u2014 three suits.\n\nIf I discard **B2**, then only two suits: Bamboo and Characters \u2014 but Half Flush requires **only one suited suit**.\n\nSo unless I eliminate one, I can't get Half Flush.\n\nSo to get Half Flush, I need to commit to **either Bamboo or Characters**.\n\nCurrently:\n- Bamboo: T567 (chow), T999 (pung) \u2192 6 tiles\n- Characters: W678 (chow), W99 \u2192 5 tiles\n\nBetter to go for **Bamboo-based Half Flush**? But W678 and W9s are separate.\n\nAlternatively, **discard a Character tile**? But I don't have extra \u2014 W9s are pair candidates.\n\nBut I have **F2 and F3**, which are honors \u2014 needed for Half Flush.\n\nBest chance: **discard B2** to remove the third suit, then hope to draw more Bamboo or convert hand.\n\nBut I still have two suits.\n\nWait \u2014 **Half Flush** allows **one suit + honors**.\n\nRight now, I have **two suited suits**: Bamboo and Characters.\n\nSo I must eliminate one.\n\nIf I keep **W678 and W99**, I keep Characters.\n\nBut I also have **T567 and T999** \u2014 that\u2019s two melds in Bamboo.\n\nSo I\u2019m stuck with two suits.\n\nUnless I can form a winning hand as-is.\n\nCurrent state: I have 14 tiles (after draw), must discard one.\n\nSo I am not winning yet.\n\nPossible waits?\n\nSuppose I keep W9 pair \u2014 then I need one more W9 to win? No \u2014 I already have two W9 \u2014 if I draw W9, I can Peng, but I would need to declare it.\n\nBut I haven't drawn yet \u2014 I am in turn to discard.\n\nSo I must discard one of the five: B2, F2, F3, W9, W9.\n\nOptions:\n\n- Discard **B2**: removes the only Dot tile, reducing suit diversity. Makes future Half Flush (if I drop Characters) slightly easier. But currently still two suits.\n\n- Discard **F2**: South Wind, prevalent wind \u2014 risky. Others may be collecting.\n\n- Discard **F3**: West Wind \u2014 less risky, not prevalent or seat wind.\n\n- Discard **W9**: breaks potential W9 pair. But I have two \u2014 discarding one leaves one, so no pair.\n\nBut perhaps I should keep W9 \u2014 because W678+W9 suggests possible **Upper Four** (6,7,8,9) \u2014 but **Upper Four** is worth 12 points, requires all tiles to be 6,7,8,9 in suited tiles.\n\nCheck:\n- T567 \u2192 has T5 \u2014 not allowed (5 < 6)\n- So **invalid** for Upper Four.\n\nSimilarly, **Lower Four**? 1,2,3,4 \u2014 no.\n\nSo no.\n\nAnother idea: **All Types** (6 points) \u2014 requires all three suits, wind, dragon.\n\nI have:\n- Three suits: yes (B2 = Dots, T = Bamboo, W = Char)\n- Wind: F2, F3 \u2014 yes\n- Dragon: none in hand or melds \u2014 opponent has Peng J1, so J1 is out.\n\nJ1, J2, J3 \u2014 unshown: J1:1, J2:1, J3:1 \u2014 all still available? Opponent discarded J3, so one J3 out.\n\nBut I don\u2019t have any dragon tile.\n\nSo cannot complete All Types unless I draw a dragon.\n\nBut dragons are rare.\n\nBetter to play safe.\n\nNow, look at unshown tiles:\n\n- T8: 0 \u2014 all four T8 are already discarded or used. So **no T8 left**.\n- B4: 0 \u2014 all gone.\n- F4: 0 \u2014 all gone.\n\nSo F4 (North Wind) \u2014 all discarded. I discarded one earlier.\n\nSince F4 is gone, **Seat Wind (North)** is not held by anyone likely \u2014 so **Seat Wind** pattern (2 points) is unlikely for others.\n\nSimilarly, F2 (South) \u2014 3 unshown, so possible.\n\nF3 (West) \u2014 3 unshown.\n\nBut I have one F3 \u2014 if I discard it, might be safe.\n\nBut better to keep F3 than F2, since F2 is prevalent.\n\nNow, which discard minimizes point loss and maximizes safety?\n\n**B2** is safest to discard because:\n- It\u2019s a simple tile (not terminal/honor)\n- Not wind/dragon\n- Only one in hand\n- From a suit not strongly represented\n- Removing it eliminates the Dots suit, simplifying hand\n\nCompared to discarding W9 \u2014 which breaks potential pair in a high-value area (W9 could be useful for All Terminals, etc.)\n\nBut W9: 3 unshown \u2014 relatively safe.\n\nHowever, **B2** is better to discard because it's the only tile of its suit.\n\nAlso, **Outside Hand** requires every meld to have terminal or honor \u2014 my T567 is all simples \u2014 so not Outside.\n\nSimilarly, **All Simples** \u2014 broken by T999 and honors.\n\nSo no.\n\nFinal evaluation: **B2** is the most expendable tile. It doesn't support any major pattern, and keeping it hinders flush attempts.\n\nReason: B2 is the only tile from the Dots suit, making it the most isolated and least useful for forming cohesive high-scoring patterns like Half Flush or Full Flush. Discarding it streamlines the hand towards potentially stronger configurations involving Bamboo and Characters with honors, while minimizing risk.\n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
